welcome back to cbs saturday morning we  begin this half hour with the global  hunger crisis triggered by the war in  ukraine on friday the united nations  world food program called for ports in  southern ukraine to be reopened in order  to export the country's surplus of grain  needed to feed millions of people around  the world the u.n estimates that the war  could cause the number of humans facing  acute hunger to rise by 47 million to  323 million worldwide ukrainian farmers  meanwhile are facing far more problems  than where to send their harvests chris  livesay reports  if an army marches on its  stomach then ukraine's farms have never  been more vital and more of a target  the piglets and cavs were screaming says  lubov's labina they were burned alive  wheat fields have become battlefields  many farmers trading in their pitchforks  for rifles as russian forces are said to  be trucking away tons of ukrainian grain  insulting the earth they leave behind  what's the risk if you were to  cultivate that land right now  it could mean death for me and my  workers  north of kiev andre kurotkov runs the  last operative farm within 30 miles do  you think that they're trying to use  hunger as a weapon  i've seen it with my own eyes he says  they've bombed our storehouses and when  they leave they steal everything they  often cut the electricity and the water  supply too now some 45 percent of  ukrainians worry about finding enough to  eat according to the world food program  karatkov says it's the same tactic of  the infamous 1933 famine when stalin  starved millions of ukrainians by  cutting off their food  this time it's also the world's food  ukraine provides tens of millions of  tons of wheat and corn globally now cut  to a trickle by russia's invasion by  land and blockade by sea but the farming  must go on  some suiting up with body armor in order  to feed the country  we go out past the checkpoints get to  work drink tea and coffee put on our  vests and go says yuri  others seen on social media use their  tractors to capture abandoned russian  armored vehicles low-hanging fruit left  by soldiers low on morale  such acts are nothing short of heroic  says kharatkov as his country fights  tooth and nail  my biggest worry is for human life my  own family  for ukraine to be free so our children  and grandchildren are by our side he  says  like so many warriors  he's met his breaking point  what's your message to the russian  soldiers who are currently  on this land  that none of them go home alive he says  and our heroes come home to their  families  for cbs saturday morning chris lives say  keeve
 

